WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.902
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll use

00:00:03.902 --> 00:00:06.852
a switch statement to match fixed choices,

00:00:06.852 --> 00:00:10.014
control each case with break, and explain the

00:00:10.014 --> 00:00:12.753
default fallback and fall-through, when

00:00:12.753 --> 00:00:15.423
execution continues into a later case.

00:00:16.066 --> 00:00:19.610
Organizing fixed choices and their exits helps

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:23.231
applications handle menu commands consistently,

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:26.774
provide useful responses to unknown input, and

00:00:26.774 --> 00:00:30.241
avoid performing an unintended second action.

00:00:30.893 --> 00:00:33.890
A library kiosk needs to show a visitor one

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:37.235
service instruction for a supplied text command.

00:00:37.235 --> 00:00:40.510
We'll first build a small information menu with

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:42.322
hours and status commands.

00:00:42.322 --> 00:00:45.249
Then we'll apply the same matching rule to

00:00:45.249 --> 00:00:46.852
borrowing and renewing.

00:00:46.852 --> 00:00:50.127
These examples display instructions; they don't

00:00:50.127 --> 00:00:52.218
access a real library account.

00:00:52.865 --> 00:00:55.657
A supported command should select its own

00:00:55.657 --> 00:00:58.789
instruction, while other text should receive a

00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:01.104
reminder of the available choices.

00:01:01.104 --> 00:01:04.236
We'll also print a readiness message after our

00:01:04.236 --> 00:01:07.232
first switch, so you can see where execution

00:01:07.232 --> 00:01:07.913
continues.

00:01:07.913 --> 00:01:10.977
Later, you'll predict the completed borrowing

00:01:10.977 --> 00:01:13.565
and renewing example before we run it.

00:01:13.605 --> 00:01:17.286
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace and start

00:01:17.286 --> 00:01:18.930
the information menu.

00:01:23.026 --> 00:01:26.014
Main is the class containing this program's

00:01:26.014 --> 00:01:29.211
code, and its main method is where the program

00:01:29.211 --> 00:01:29.698
starts.

00:01:29.698 --> 00:01:32.756
We'll put our statements inside that method.

00:01:32.756 --> 00:01:35.396
The command variable will store hours.

00:01:35.396 --> 00:01:38.732
A switch matches that one supplied value against

00:01:38.732 --> 00:01:41.790
fixed choices; for ranges or combinations of

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:45.126
facts, the earlier if statement is a better fit.

00:01:55.403 --> 00:01:58.115
Command is the selector, the value being

00:01:58.115 --> 00:01:58.658
matched.

00:01:58.658 --> 00:02:01.709
We need an entry point for the hours request.

00:02:01.709 --> 00:02:04.556
The case keyword introduces its fixed text

00:02:04.556 --> 00:02:07.675
label, and the colon marks where that choice's

00:02:07.675 --> 00:02:08.828
statements begin.

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:11.947
We'll display the opening time, then use break

00:02:11.947 --> 00:02:13.303
to leave the switch.

00:02:23.286 --> 00:02:26.204
That break prevents the hours request from

00:02:26.204 --> 00:02:29.260
continuing into another service instruction.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.455
We'll add a status choice for the service desk

00:02:32.455 --> 00:02:35.373
and a default fallback for unmatched text.

00:02:35.373 --> 00:02:37.457
Default has no value to match.

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:40.513
It identifies the starting point to use when

00:02:40.513 --> 00:02:42.736
none of the named cases matches.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:15.705
Our default group is last, so reaching its end

00:03:15.705 --> 00:03:18.091
reaches the switch's closing brace.

00:03:18.091 --> 00:03:21.294
The final readiness print is outside the switch

00:03:21.294 --> 00:03:22.385
but inside main.

00:03:22.385 --> 00:03:25.452
With hours, Java should enter the first case,

00:03:25.452 --> 00:03:28.383
display the opening time, and break to that

00:03:28.383 --> 00:03:29.473
readiness print.

00:03:29.473 --> 00:03:32.404
Let's run the complete program to check the

00:03:32.404 --> 00:03:32.745
path.

00:03:38.302 --> 00:03:41.602
We see the opening-time instruction followed by

00:03:41.602 --> 00:03:42.374
Menu ready.

00:03:42.374 --> 00:03:45.182
Break ended the switch, not the program.

00:03:45.182 --> 00:03:48.552
The status and default instructions did not run.

00:03:48.552 --> 00:03:51.781
Notice the braces around the switch body: case

00:03:51.781 --> 00:03:55.081
labels mark entry points, but they don't create

00:03:55.081 --> 00:03:57.327
separate blocks or local scopes.

00:03:57.417 --> 00:03:59.280
The supported path works.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.483
We also need evidence for input outside the

00:04:02.483 --> 00:04:02.856
list.

00:04:02.856 --> 00:04:06.432
We'll change only the command to events, leaving

00:04:06.432 --> 00:04:08.667
every case and exit unchanged.

00:04:12.992 --> 00:04:16.080
Events matches neither hours nor status.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.091
Java should start at default, print the

00:04:19.091 --> 00:04:22.488
available choices, and then continue to Menu

00:04:22.488 --> 00:04:22.951
ready.

00:04:22.951 --> 00:04:25.267
Let's run this unmatched case.

00:04:29.812 --> 00:04:33.069
The fallback reminder prints, followed by the

00:04:33.069 --> 00:04:34.372
readiness message.

00:04:34.372 --> 00:04:37.702
The fallback is selected because no named case

00:04:37.702 --> 00:04:38.281
matches.

00:04:38.281 --> 00:04:41.683
String matching uses text contents and respects

00:04:41.683 --> 00:04:43.348
letter case and spaces.

00:04:43.348 --> 00:04:46.389
A label is an exact choice, not a test for

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:49.501
whether one word appears somewhere inside a

00:04:49.501 --> 00:04:50.587
longer command.

00:04:50.671 --> 00:04:54.016
Matching the right entry point is only half the

00:04:54.016 --> 00:04:54.300
job.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:57.502
We'll restore hours, then deliberately remove

00:04:57.502 --> 00:04:58.214
its break.

00:04:58.214 --> 00:05:01.274
The program will still compile, but it will

00:05:01.274 --> 00:05:04.619
violate our rule of one service instruction per

00:05:04.619 --> 00:05:05.188
command.

00:05:05.188 --> 00:05:08.461
Let's make those two small edits so we can see

00:05:08.461 --> 00:05:08.746
why.

00:05:12.541 --> 00:05:15.618
Execution will enter the hours case and print

00:05:15.618 --> 00:05:16.713
its instruction.

00:05:16.713 --> 00:05:19.653
Without that break, it keeps going into the

00:05:19.653 --> 00:05:22.662
statements under status, without testing the

00:05:22.662 --> 00:05:23.962
status label again.

00:05:23.962 --> 00:05:25.398
This is fall-through.

00:05:25.398 --> 00:05:28.612
The break after the status message will finally

00:05:28.612 --> 00:05:29.775
leave the switch.

00:05:29.775 --> 00:05:32.920
Let's run the faulty version and inspect every

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:33.741
output line.

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:41.303
Two service instructions appear before Menu

00:05:41.303 --> 00:05:41.740
ready.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:45.163
The selector never changed to status; execution

00:05:45.163 --> 00:05:47.494
simply continued past its label.

00:05:47.494 --> 00:05:50.917
The program ran successfully, but the result is

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:52.811
wrong for our requirement.

00:05:52.811 --> 00:05:55.869
Testing only status would have missed this

00:05:55.869 --> 00:05:59.147
earlier missing break, so test each supported

00:05:59.147 --> 00:06:00.021
entry point.

00:06:00.107 --> 00:06:03.194
We'll repair the hours action by restoring its

00:06:03.194 --> 00:06:06.416
print statement together with the missing break.

00:06:06.416 --> 00:06:09.302
The statement after the switch will stay in

00:06:09.302 --> 00:06:12.524
place, so the corrected run can still show where

00:06:12.524 --> 00:06:13.732
control continues.

00:06:23.627 --> 00:06:26.813
Hours now prints its instruction and exits

00:06:26.813 --> 00:06:28.634
before the status label.

00:06:28.634 --> 00:06:32.048
Let's rerun that same input to check that the

00:06:32.048 --> 00:06:34.627
extra service instruction is gone.

00:06:39.094 --> 00:06:42.096
The repaired version prints only the hours

00:06:42.096 --> 00:06:44.026
instruction and Menu ready.

00:06:44.026 --> 00:06:47.457
We changed the exit, not the selected command or

00:06:47.457 --> 00:06:48.315
its message.

00:06:48.315 --> 00:06:51.388
That comparison identifies the cause of the

00:06:51.388 --> 00:06:52.175
extra line.

00:06:52.175 --> 00:06:54.962
Fall-through can be deliberate in other

00:06:54.962 --> 00:06:57.893
programs, but it doesn't suit this menu's

00:06:57.893 --> 00:06:59.394
one-instruction rule.

00:06:59.521 --> 00:07:02.691
We'll now replace the information menu with the

00:07:02.691 --> 00:07:05.389
lesson's borrowing and renewing program.

00:07:05.389 --> 00:07:08.424
It uses the same structure and matching rule,

00:07:08.424 --> 00:07:11.527
with a different set of commands and messages.

00:07:11.527 --> 00:07:14.494
This final version has no separate readiness

00:07:14.494 --> 00:07:14.899
print.

00:07:14.899 --> 00:07:17.934
Read the choices as we build it, then predict

00:07:17.934 --> 00:07:18.946
which one runs.

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:31.390
The supplied command is renew.

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:34.400
We'll add the two supported case labels and

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.060
their exits, followed by the fallback.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:40.070
Each print belongs to the label immediately

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:40.700
above it.

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:43.920
Use the worked information menu to trace where

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.440
execution begins and where it stops.

00:08:17.047 --> 00:08:20.468
Before we run, write the exact output you expect

00:08:20.468 --> 00:08:23.462
and name the label where execution begins.

00:08:23.462 --> 00:08:26.598
Explain whether either of the other messages

00:08:26.598 --> 00:08:27.382
will print.

00:08:27.382 --> 00:08:30.447
Pause the video here if you need more time.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.357
Let's click Run and compare the actual

00:08:41.357 --> 00:08:43.820
instruction with your prediction.

00:08:48.159 --> 00:08:50.893
The program prints Open your loan list.

00:08:50.893 --> 00:08:54.047
Renew matches the second case, so the earlier

00:08:54.047 --> 00:08:56.150
borrow statements are skipped.

00:08:56.150 --> 00:08:59.094
After the selected print, break leaves the

00:08:59.094 --> 00:09:00.636
switch before default.

00:09:00.636 --> 00:09:03.721
There is no later print in main, so this run

00:09:03.721 --> 00:09:06.034
produces exactly one instruction.

00:09:06.194 --> 00:09:09.389
We've built a fixed-command choice, checked an

00:09:09.389 --> 00:09:12.375
unmatched command, and repaired an unwanted

00:09:12.375 --> 00:09:13.695
second instruction.

00:09:13.695 --> 00:09:16.751
A switch chooses an entry point, case labels

00:09:16.751 --> 00:09:19.876
name the supported values, default handles an

00:09:19.876 --> 00:09:23.002
unmatched value, and break controls the exit.

00:09:23.002 --> 00:09:25.710
Together, those parts give this kiosk a

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:27.169
predictable response.

00:09:28.058 --> 00:09:30.964
Now use the notebook to build its related

00:09:30.964 --> 00:09:32.169
library selector.

00:09:32.169 --> 00:09:35.004
Test every supported command, an unknown

00:09:35.004 --> 00:09:38.264
command, and the stated change in letter case.

00:09:38.264 --> 00:09:41.241
Compare both the message and the number of

00:09:41.241 --> 00:09:41.666
lines.

00:09:41.666 --> 00:09:44.926
Explain why these fixed choices suit a switch,

00:09:44.926 --> 00:09:48.257
and why a range-based rule would call for an if

00:09:48.257 --> 00:09:49.533
condition instead.
